% per-class accuracy ceiling and its taxonomic-rollup graceful-degradation
% property vs.\ a single-stage detector's single forward pass, unified
% latency budget, and straightforward embedded export path; the ensemble's
% two-stage latency (localize, then crop-and-classify per detection) vs.\ a
% single detector's fixed-cost inference regardless of instance count. This
% comparison is the literature grounding for why the thesis trains a single
% end-to-end detector rather than attempting to compress or port the ensemble
% itself, stated concretely in \Cref{sec:model_selection}.

\subsection{Model Selection: Practical Constraints}\label{sec:model_selection}
% TODO: unlike the rest of this chapter, this subsection is not a literature
