% constraints established above and elsewhere in this chapter/Methods:
% (a) the YOLOv5 commercial-licensing cutoff at commit \texttt{5cdad89} (see
% CLAUDE.md) fixed which YOLOv5 codebase revision was usable at all; (b) the
% $200$--$250$-class ceiling for nano-scale backbones
% (Methods \Cref{sec:target_class_definition}) and the QCS605/Hexagon 685
% budget ruled out the MegaDetector+SpeciesNet ensemble
% (\Cref{sec:lit_yolo_vs_ensemble}) as a deployable target without a compression
% step this thesis does not undertake; (c) time budget (\Cref{chapter:results}'s
% reduced KD/embedded scope) ruled out a comparative architecture search across
% newer YOLO releases (\Cref{sec:lit_yolo_family}) or non-YOLO nano detectors
% (NanoDet, PicoDet, EfficientDet-Lite) that were considered early in the
% project (docs/2026-03-10\_object-detection-models-for-embedded-systems.md)
% but not benchmarked empirically. State plainly that \textit{YOLOv5s} was
% therefore a practical, constraint-satisfying choice rather than the winner of
% an architecture comparison, and that this is a named limitation
% (\Cref{sec:limitations}), not a hidden one.

